course: dictionary
lesson: 15302
title: Slain and slay
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Slain is a past form of the verb slay, which means to kill. Slay goes slay, slew, slain. We use slain with a helper word like «was» or «has been»: «The dragon was slain by the brave knight.»
explain: Slain can also describe a noun, like an adjective: «The slain soldiers were remembered at the ceremony.» Here slain tells you which soldiers: the ones who had been killed.
explain: Look at the words around it. Slain is used in old stories, myths and the news, where the meaning is serious. «David slew Goliath» uses slew, the simple past form, with no helper word.
explain: Be careful with slang. Friends might say «You slayed in that dance show!» That means you did something brilliantly, not that you killed anyone. Check the context to see which meaning fits.
fact: Slay is a verb, and its forms are slay, slew, slain and slaying.
fact: «Slain» needs a helper word, such as «was» or «has been»: «The dragon was slain.»
fact: «Slain» can also describe people, as in «the slain soldiers», meaning those who were killed.
fact: «Slay» in modern slang means to do something brilliantly, as in «You slayed!»
fact: Old stories and the news use slain for serious killing, so the context tells you which meaning is right.
exercise: The dragon was ___ by the brave knight. | slew | slaying | *slain | slays
exercise: «The slain soldiers were remembered at the ceremony.» What does slain mean here? | Asleep in their tents | Found safe and well | Painted in bright colours | *Killed
exercise: «David slew Goliath.» Find and copy the word that is the simple past form of slay. | and | *slew | David | Goliath
test: The hero ___ the giant, and the village cheered. | slain | slaying | *slew | slays
test: «You slayed in that dance show!» What does slayed mean in this slang sentence? | Was very tired afterwards | *Did something brilliantly | Fell over on stage | Killed someone
exam: Which sentence uses slain correctly? | «The slain is a colour of the sea.» | *«The dragon was slain by the brave knight.» | «The knight slain the dragon yesterday.»
